  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}-4(4)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)(t+1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14094076/assets/002f547b882940d087d6ed9d228334ec163931cfea0665eafdfe8b6ec8d863da.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$",
